        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If no soap is coming out, the wrong nozzle is almost certainly fitted. The detergent siphon only engages at low pressure, so any high-pressure tip stops it drawing entirely, and the black soap nozzle is the only one that will let it work. After that, check the siphon tube, its filter, and the tank selector on dual-tank machines.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The method is pre-wet, apply from the bottom up, dwell for several minutes without drying, then rinse from the top down. Applying downward onto dry siding leaves streaks that are harder to remove than the dirt was, and rinsing upward wastes the work. On vertical surfaces a foam cannon extends dwell from seconds to minutes, which is the whole point.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose products formulated for pressure washers, never bleach unless the machine permits it, and never dish soap on a car. Then flush plain water through the system for a minute at the end of every session, because dried detergent in the injector is the usual reason a working soap system quietly stops.
          </p>
        </div>


